/-!
# Depth of regular-sequence quotients of a regular local ring

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tags 00NQ (lemma-regular-ring-CM),
02JN (lemma-reformulate-CM), and 00LK (lemma-bound-depth)
(blueprint: lem-trf-regular-local-regular-sequence-depth).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

namespace IsRegularLocalRing

/-- Every prefix quotient of a regular sequence in the maximal ideal of a regular
local ring is nonzero Noetherian local, with maximal ideal the image of the original
maximal ideal, and has depth equal to its Krull dimension. The list length is `c`;
`i = 0` includes the original ring via `R / 0`, and `i = rs.length` gives the full quotient.
Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tags 00NQ, 02JN, and 00LK
(blueprint: lem-trf-regular-local-regular-sequence-depth).

Proof, following the node's four steps:
1. For `I_i = Ideal.ofList (rs.take i)`, membership of every entry in the maximal ideal
   gives `I_i ≤ maximalIdeal R`. The quotient is nonzero and local, with maximal ideal
   the image of `maximalIdeal R`, by lem-weil-pbc-quotient-local-structure. Since a regular
   local ring is Noetherian, lem-weil-pbc-surjective-image-noetherian makes it Noetherian.
2. Induct on `i` to show depth at least dimension. At zero choose `dim R` generators of
   the maximal ideal. By lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-generators-regular-sequence they form
   a regular sequence. The maximal ideal is proper, so def-weil-pbc-depth gives the lower
   bound, also when the dimension is zero and the generator list is empty.
3. At the next step the image of the next entry is in the quotient maximal ideal and
   is a nonzerodivisor by def-weil-pbc-regular-sequence. Apply
   lem-weil-pbc-cm-local-quotient-nonzerodivisor-cm to this Noetherian local ring, using
   the induction hypothesis. Its quotient by that entry has depth at least its dimension.
   Identify this double quotient with the next prefix quotient by sending the double
   class of `r` to its class modulo `I_i + (rs[i])`. The inverse sends a class to its
   double class. These maps are well-defined by the ideal equality and inverse on every
   representative. They preserve maximal ideals and prime chains, hence dimension;
   they conjugate multiplication on all successive quotients, hence preserve regular
   sequences and depth. This proves the induction without assuming quotient regularity.
4. Apply lem-weil-pbc-depth-le-support-dimension to each quotient as its own finite
   module, generated by one. Its support is the whole prime spectrum: if `1/1 = 0`
   at a prime, some element outside that prime annihilates one, forcing that element
   to be zero, a contradiction. Thus depth is at most ring dimension. Antisymmetry
   gives equality. Taking the full list proves the last assertion; the empty list
   uses just the base case and this upper bound.
-/
theorem regular_sequence_quotients_depth_eq
    (R : Type u) [CommRing R] [IsRegularLocalRing R] (rs : List R)
    (hmem : ∀ x ∈ rs, x ∈ IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R)
    (hregular : RingTheory.Sequence.IsRegular R rs) :
    ∀ i : ℕ, i ≤ rs.length →
      Nontrivial (R ⧸ Ideal.ofList (rs.take i)) ∧
      IsNoetherianRing (R ⧸ Ideal.ofList (rs.take i)) ∧
      ∃ hQ : IsLocalRing (R ⧸ Ideal.ofList (rs.take i)),
        @IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal (R ⧸ Ideal.ofList (rs.take i)) _ hQ =
          (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R).map (Ideal.Quotient.mk (Ideal.ofList (rs.take i))) ∧
        (@IsLocalRing.depth (R ⧸ Ideal.ofList (rs.take i)) _ hQ
          (R ⧸ Ideal.ofList (rs.take i)) _ _ _ : WithBot ℕ∞) =
          ringKrullDim (R ⧸ Ideal.ofList (rs.take i)) := by
  sorry

end IsRegularLocalRing
